<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wounded Knee Massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Wounded Knee</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wounded Knee Massacre took place on 29 December 1890 near Wounded Knee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> on the Lakota Pine Ridge Indian Reservation in South Dakota, during the U.S. Army's Pine Ridge Campaign. The 7th Cavalry Regiment had surrounded the camp of Spotted Elk's band of Miniconjou Lakota and 38 Hunkpapa Lakota, supported by four Hotchkiss mountain guns, amid settlers' fears of the Ghost Dance. During an attempt to disarm the Lakota a rifle went off, the firing became indiscriminate, and between 250 and 300 Lakota were killed, along with 25 soldiers. In 1990 both houses of Congress expressed deep regret for the massacre.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the years before the conflict the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government continued to seize Lakota lands. The bison herds had been hunted almost to extinction, and treaty promises to protect reservation land from settlers and gold miners were not kept. News spread of a Paiute prophet, Wovoka, founder of the Ghost Dance religion, who had a vision that Jesus Christ had returned to Earth as a Native American. The Ghost Dance was understood to revitalise pre-colonial life, and by 1890 nearly one third of the Lakota had joined it. Settlers feared it signified a dangerous Sioux resurgence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Spotted Elk of the Miniconjou and 350 followers were travelling to the Pine Ridge Agency on 28 December 1890 when Major Samuel M. Whitside's detachment of the 7th Cavalry met them and escorted them to Wounded Knee Creek. Colonel James W. Forsyth arrived that evening with the rest of the regiment, bringing the troopers to 500 against 350 Lakota, of whom 120 were men and 230 women and children. The troopers surrounded the camp and set up four Hotchkiss mountain guns.</p><p>At daybreak on 29 December Forsyth ordered the Lakota to surrender their weapons. A search of the camp took 38 rifles. In one account a deaf man named Black Coyote was reluctant to give up his rifle and it went off, and about five young men with concealed weapons threw off their blankets and fired. The firing then became indiscriminate, and many Lakota had already been disarmed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After a three-day blizzard, civilians hired by the army buried the frozen dead in a mass grave. Miles denounced Forsyth and relieved him of command, and a Court of Inquiry criticised his dispositions but otherwise cleared him. The army awarded 31 Medals of Honor for the campaign, 19 of them specifically for Wounded Knee, and in 2001 the National Congress of American Indians called for them to be rescinded. Wounded Knee is generally considered the end of the long series of conflicts known as the Indian Wars. The battlefield became a National Historic Landmark in 1965.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wounded Knee Massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They defended it against the United States, winning at the Little Bighorn in 1876, but were confined to reservations, and the <b>Wounded Knee Massacre</b> of 1890 became a symbol of US policy.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Wounded Knee Massacre</b> was not the end of the Ghost Dance.</blockquote><a href="/movement/ghost-dance">Ghost Dance · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
